{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Usability Testing and Learning Evidence","title":"Analyzing Feedback Without Overclaiming","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting analyzing feedback without overclaiming to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence","ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"06","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/05/05"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_5_6_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_5_6_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_5_6_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_5_6_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming_calibration"]},"content":"# Analyzing Feedback Without Overclaiming\n\n![Analyzing Feedback Without Overclaiming textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/05-06-analyzing-feedback-without-overclaiming.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nFormative usability tests are built to surface problems cheaply, not to prove things conclusively, and the most damaging mistake at the analysis stage is forgetting that bargain. Five participants can reveal that something is worth fixing; they cannot prove that a feature \"fails\" or that a population \"hates\" it. This section is about calibration: scaling the strength of a claim to the strength of the evidence, so a small qualitative signal becomes a candidate problem to verify rather than a verdict to act on. Overclaiming is seductive because a sharp conclusion feels more useful than a hedged one, but a confident claim built on thin data sends a team to redesign the wrong thing.\n\nIn AIL 606 the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes is revised on the basis of what testing actually showed, and the analysis must keep claim strength honest. A finding that says more than the data can support is not a strong finding; it is a liability dressed as a result.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Scale claim strength to sample size and evidence type, distinguishing a candidate issue from a confirmed defect.\n- Replace evaluative language (hate, fails, proves) with counts of what was actually observed.\n- Separate a frequent signal from a severe one, and name which the evidence supports.\n- State the verification step a finding needs before it justifies a costly design change.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nAfter a five-person test of an onboarding storyboard, a designer writes: \"Users hate the progress bar. We proved it fails and should remove it.\" The sentence is decisive, quotable, and wrong on every count. \"Users\" generalizes from five people; \"hate\" and \"fails\" replace observation with emotion; \"proved\" claims certainty a formative test cannot deliver; and \"remove it\" is a product decision riding on all three errors.\n\nWhat actually happened was smaller and more useful: two participants commented that the progress-bar labels were ambiguous, one ignored it, and nobody failed the task because of it. That is a candidate issue worth a clearer label and a re-test, not a defect worth deletion. The overclaim did not just exaggerate; it pointed the next iteration at the wrong response.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nCalibration means choosing the verb that matches the evidence. The same observation can support a weak or a strong claim, and the analyst's job is to pick the honest one.\n\n| Evidence | Defensible claim | Overclaim to avoid |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 2 of 5 commented, 0 failed | \"Candidate label issue; verify\" | \"Users hate it; it fails\" |\n| Same issue across varied participants | \"Likely problem worth fixing\" | \"Proven defect\" |\n| One vivid complaint | \"A signal to watch\" | \"The main problem\" |\n\nTwo distinctions keep claims calibrated. The first is observation versus emotion: \"hate\" and \"fails\" are interpretations, while \"two negative comments, zero task failures\" is what occurred; only the latter can be defended. The second is frequency versus severity: an issue many people hit lightly is different from an issue one person hit hard, and conflating them (\"everyone struggled\") hides which response the design needs. **UDL learner variability** sharpens this, because a problem that appears only in one subgroup may be severe for them and invisible in the aggregate, so the count must say who, not just how many.\n\nThere is also a theory reason to slow down. **Cognitive load theory** reminds you that a single complaint about effort might reflect productive germane load (the learner thinking hard) rather than a flaw, so a comment is a prompt to investigate, not a confirmed problem. And **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)** keeps the analysis tied to whether the storyboard supports the intended learning action, not to whether participants found it pleasant; a well-liked frame that fails the learning goal is still a problem the comments may have missed entirely.\n\n## Worked Example: Rewriting a Finding So It Does Not Overclaim\n\nA designer summarizes results after a 5-participant test of an onboarding storyboard.\n\n**Before (overclaimed finding):**\n\n> Finding: Users hate the progress bar. We proved the progress bar fails and should remove it from the product.\n\nWalk through how to scale the claim to the evidence:\n\n1. **Count what actually happened.** 2 of 5 participants commented negatively on the progress bar; 1 ignored it; 2 used it without comment.\n2. **Distinguish observation from preference.** \"Hate\" and \"fails\" are not what was observed; what was observed is two negative comments and no completion failures.\n3. **Match the claim strength to the sample.** Five participants in a formative test can surface candidate problems, not population-level proof.\n4. **State the finding with its uncertainty and a next step.**\n\n**After (calibrated finding):**\n\n> Finding: In this 5-participant formative session, 2 participants found the progress-bar labels ambiguous; no participant failed the task because of it. This is a candidate issue, not a confirmed defect. Next step: add a clearer label and re-test with a larger, more varied sample before deciding to remove it.\n\nThe after version is defensible because the claim does not exceed what five sessions can show, and it names a verification step. The before version overgeneralized a small qualitative signal into proof and a product decision, the central failure this section guards against.\n\nA count only licenses a claim as strong as the proportion and the sample behind it. Adjust how many of how many participants commented, and how many actually failed the task, to see why the same complaint reads as a candidate signal at five participants but never as proof.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"How much can this count actually license?\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"sample\",\"label\":\"Participants tested\",\"min\":3,\"max\":30,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"people\",\"default\":5},{\"name\":\"commented\",\"label\":\"Participants who commented negatively\",\"min\":0,\"max\":30,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"people\",\"default\":2},{\"name\":\"failed\",\"label\":\"Participants who FAILED the task because of it\",\"min\":0,\"max\":30,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"people\",\"default\":0}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Share commenting\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"100 * commented / sample\"},{\"label\":\"Share with task failure\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"100 * failed / sample\"},{\"label\":\"One participant is worth\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"100 / sample\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"sample\",\"output\":2}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nRead the third output: at five participants one person swings the share by 20 points, so a 40 percent comment rate is two people, far too thin to support hate or fails. Drive failures to zero and notice that no proportion of comments converts a candidate issue into a confirmed defect; only a larger, varied re-test with actual task failures could do that.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\n<inline-check question=\"Two of five participants commented that the progress-bar labels were ambiguous and nobody failed the task. Which write-up is calibrated to that evidence?\" explanation=\"Five participants in a formative test surface candidate problems, not population-level proof, and two negative comments with zero failures is a signal to verify, not a verdict. Users hate it and proves it fails both upgrade the verb past the count; deleting on this evidence skips the re-test the honest response requires.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Users hate the progress bar; we proved it fails\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Candidate label issue: two of five flagged it, none failed; clarify the label and re-test before removing\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The progress bar is the products main problem and should be cut now\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No issue exists since nobody failed the task\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nState only what the count supports, then name the verification a stronger claim would require before you act on it. If a finding uses \"proves,\" \"all,\" \"hate,\" or any verdict, replace it with the number behind it and ask whether that number licenses the action you are about to take.\n\nBefore locking the analysis into your storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, check three things:\n\n1. **Calibration check:** Does the claim's strength match the sample size and evidence type?\n2. **Language check:** Have you replaced emotion words with counts of observed behavior?\n3. **Verification check:** For any costly change, have you named the re-test that would confirm the issue first?\n\nIf any check fails, the finding asserts more than five sessions can defend.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe frequent error is treating a vivid anecdote as the headline; one memorable complaint is easy to overweight precisely because it is memorable, not because it is representative. A second error is sliding from \"a problem appeared\" to \"the feature failed,\" which jumps from a signal to a verdict in one word. A third is removing a feature on formative evidence alone, when the honest response to a candidate issue is to revise and re-test, not to delete.\n\nA formative test earns the right to say \"worth checking,\" rarely the right to say \"proven.\"\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTrace one signal from raw comments to a calibrated finding. In a six-person storyboard test, three participants hesitate at a frame where narration and on-screen text say slightly different things. The tempting write-up is \"the narration confuses everyone.\" The calibrated version counts and locates: \"Three of six hesitated at frame 4, where audio and caption differed; none abandoned.\" That hesitation has a named mechanism in **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**, the redundancy effect, so the finding is not just a complaint but a testable hypothesis about why the frame underperformed.\n\nNow decide the response proportional to the evidence. Because no one failed and the cause is identifiable, the honest move is to align the narration and caption in the storyboard and re-test, not to declare the frame broken. **UDL learner variability** adds a check: were the three who hesitated the multilingual participants, for whom mismatched audio and text is a heavier load? If so, the finding tightens to who is affected and why, which is more useful, and more defensible, than the sweeping \"everyone was confused\" the raw comments first suggested.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a findings list, keep one column for the claim as written and one for the count that backs it, then flag every claim whose verb outruns its number. The gap between the verb and the count is the overclaim, and it is usually where a team is about to spend effort it cannot justify.\n\nA strong reader of an analysis can answer four questions:\n\n1. What exact count or behavior supports this claim?\n2. Is the verb (candidate, likely, proven) calibrated to that evidence?\n3. Is the issue frequent, severe, or both, and for whom?\n4. What verification would a costlier response require before acting?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe analysis is strong when a reviewer can trace every claim back to a number and agree the claim says neither more nor less than that number allows. Treat the verb in each finding as the load-bearing element; \"proves\" and \"fails\" quietly upgrade a candidate signal into a mandate. Use the annotation tools to flag any finding stated in emotion words, and ask BigAL to compare your claims against the calibration check rather than to make the findings sound more conclusive.\n\nA useful closing line reads: \"I rewrote 'users hate the progress bar' as 'two of five found the labels ambiguous, none failed, verify before removing' because the original overclaimed a five-person signal as proof; I rejected the suggestion to cut the feature now, since formative evidence supports a re-test, not deletion.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same calibration discipline to interpreting student course evaluations, where a handful of strong comments tempt the same overreach. Three pointed complaints about an assignment are a candidate issue worth examining, not proof that \"students hate the course,\" exactly as two progress-bar comments were a signal, not a verdict. The structure transfers (count it, calibrate the verb, name the verification), but the emotional pull of a sharp negative comment is even stronger in evaluations. A reader who lets the loudest comment set policy has made the same mistake as the designer who deleted a feature on five sessions.\n\nWork the evaluation-overreach dilemma below. A 28-student course returns three vivid complaints about one assignment, and every choice tests whether you calibrate the verb to the count.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Reading three sharp course-eval complaints\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Three of 28 students wrote pointed complaints that an assignment was confusing. A colleague says, students hate this assignment, cut it. Do you act on that reading?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, the complaints are vivid and specific, cut the assignment\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Three of 28 is a candidate signal, and hate is emotion replacing a count. Cutting on this overweights the loudest voices and may remove a productive task.\"},{\"label\":\"No, restate it as a count and look for a mechanism\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: 3 of 28 flagged it is a candidate issue to investigate, not proof. Calibrate the verb before deciding anything.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"You cut the assignment. The next cohort scores lower on the linked outcome. What went wrong?\",\"feedback\":\"You let three vivid comments set policy and removed a task that may have carried germane load. The honest move was to investigate and revise, not delete on a thin signal.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"You reread the three comments: all three found the instructions ambiguous, none said the work was pointless. What is the calibrated finding and next step?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The assignment fails; replace it\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Fails outruns the count. Ambiguous instructions are a fixable signal, not a verdict that the assignment is broken.\"},{\"label\":\"Candidate instruction-clarity issue; clarify the prompt and watch next cohort\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the verb matches the evidence and you name the verification before any costly change.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"You replaced a working assignment over an instruction-wording problem. Was that proportional?\",\"feedback\":\"No. The evidence supported clarifying the instructions, not replacing the task. You spent effort the count could not justify.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"State the finding you would record.\",\"feedback\":\"In this 28-student cohort, 3 students found the assignment instructions ambiguous; none called the task itself unhelpful. Candidate clarity issue: revise the prompt wording and monitor the next cohorts comments and outcome before larger changes.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/05-06-analyzing-feedback-without-overclaiming-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"05.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 05.06. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm each finding is backed by a count, uses a verb calibrated to that count, names frequency and severity, and states the verification any costly change would need.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming\", \"ail606_supplement_usability_testing_and_learning_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_6_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming","stem":"After a 5-participant test, a designer writes \"we proved the progress bar fails.\" Why is this an overclaim?","options":["Five participants is fine, but the word 'proved' is just informal.","A small formative session can surface candidate problems, not prove a population-level defect; 'proved' exceeds what 5 sessions can show.","Progress bars cannot be tested for usability.","The finding should have been about satisfaction instead."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Formative tests with a handful of users reveal candidate issues; claiming proof overstates what the evidence can support.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_6_small_sample_as_proof","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_6_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming","stem":"Two of five participants commented negatively on a label; none failed the task. Which finding is calibrated to the evidence?","options":["Users hate the label and it must be removed.","The label is a confirmed defect across all users.","In this session, 2 of 5 found the label ambiguous and none failed the task; treat it as a candidate issue to re-test, not a confirmed defect.","The label is fine since no one failed the task."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A calibrated finding reports the count, separates comment from failure, and names it as a candidate issue pending re-test.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_6_preference_as_failure","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_6_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"An AI tool clusters your session comments and outputs confident conclusions like \"users find navigation unintuitive (high confidence).\" What is the defensible boundary?","options":["Adopt the AI confidence labels as your findings.","Use the clustering to organize comments, but set your own claim strength based on counts and whether tasks actually failed, not the AI's confidence wording.","Reject the clusters because AI cannot read comments.","Report only the AI conclusions to save space."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI grouping can speed organization, but the designer must calibrate the claim to the observed counts and outcomes, not to AI-asserted confidence.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_6_ai_confidence_as_evidence","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_5_6_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming","stem":"One participant suggested adding a tutorial video; the team writes \"users want video tutorials\" and plans to build a video library. What is the reasoning flaw?","options":["Video tutorials are always a bad idea.","A single suggestion is generalized to 'users' and escalated into a large build decision the evidence does not support.","They should have asked the participant to justify the suggestion.","The team needed to test the video before the prototype."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Treating one person's preference as a population need, then scaling a major decision on it, overclaims well beyond the evidence.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_5_6_single_voice_generalized","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_analyzing_feedback_without_overclaiming"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"3480ee33320e26f55a7465c1289364f231c83b56e66e6e9b6bcc8744240fe572","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}